        {
          "id": "F1_7",
          "source_id": "part1",
          "source_sha256": "677e869acdebac4c68091e789f44088b14725a355381080260364e1ba4950ebd",
          "start": 6355,
          "end": 6791,
          "quote": "Fundamentally, it\ndoes two things.\nOne, performs calculations.\nIt performs a lot\nof calculations.\nComputers these days\nare really, really fast,\na billion calculations per\nsecond is probably not far off.\nIt performs these\ncalculations and it\nhas to store them somewhere.\nStores them in computer memory.\nSo a computer also has\nto remember results.\nAnd these days, it's not\nuncommon to find computers\nwith hundreds of\ngigabytes of storage.",
          "topic": "计算机的基本功能",
          "claim": "Ana Bell 认为计算机从根本上做两件事：执行大量计算，并将结果存储在计算机内存中；如今计算机速度极快，每秒十亿次计算可能并不夸张，拥有数百 GB 存储也很常见。",
          "evidence_indices": [
            273,
            274,
            275,
            276,
            277,
            278,
            279,
            280,
            281,
            282,
            283,
            284,
            285,
            286,
            287,
            288,
            289,
            290,
            291
          ],
          "attribution": "source",
          "importance": "core",
          "attribution_start": null,
          "attribution_end": null
        },
        {
          "id": "F1_8",
          "source_id": "part1",
          "source_sha256": "677e869acdebac4c68091e789f44088b14725a355381080260364e1ba4950ebd",
          "start": 6792,
          "end": 7288,
          "quote": "The kinds of calculations\nthat computers do,\nthere are two kinds.\nOne are calculations that\nare built into the language.\nThese are the very\nlow level types\nof calculations,\nthings like addition,\nsubtraction,\nmultiplication, and so on.\nAnd once you have\na language that\nhas these primitive calculation\ntypes, you, as a programmer,\ncan put these types\ntogether and then define\nyour own calculations.\nYou can create new\ntypes of calculations.\nAnd the computer will be able\nto perform those, as well.",
          "topic": "计算的两种类型",
          "claim": "Ana Bell 指出计算机所做的计算有两种：一种是语言内置的非常低层的计算，如加法、减法、乘法等；程序员可以将这些原始计算类型组合起来，定义自己的新计算类型，计算机也能执行这些新计算。",
          "evidence_indices": [
            292,
            293,
            294,
            295,
            296,
            297,
            298,
            299,
            300,
            301,
            302,
            303,
            304,
            305,
            306,
            307,
            308,
            309,
            310,
            311,
            312,
            313
          ],
          "attribution": "source",
          "importance": "core",
          "attribution_start": null,
          "attribution_end": null
        },
        {
          "id": "F1_9",
          "source_id": "part1",
          "source_sha256": "677e869acdebac4c68091e789f44088b14725a355381080260364e1ba4950ebd",
          "start": 7289,
          "end": 7689,
          "quote": "I think, one thing\nI want to stress--\nand we're going to\ncome back to this\nagain during this entire\nlecture, actually--\nis computers only know\nwhat you tell them.\nComputers only do what\nyou tell them to do.\nThey're not magical.\nThey don't have a mind.\nThey just know how to\nperform calculations really,\nreally quickly.\nBut you have to tell them\nwhat calculations to do.\nComputers don't know anything.",
          "topic": "计算机的局限",
          "claim": "Ana Bell 强调计算机只知道自己被告知的内容，只做你让它做的事，它们并不神奇、没有心智，只是能非常快速地执行计算，但你必须告诉它们要做什么计算。",
          "evidence_indices": [
            314,
            315,
            316,
            317,
            318,
            319,
            320,
            321,
            322,
            323,
            324,
            325,
            326,
            327,
            328,
            329,
            330,
            331
          ],
          "attribution": "source",
          "importance": "core",
          "attribution_start": null,
          "attribution_end": null
        },
        {
          "id": "F1_10",
          "source_id": "part1",
          "source_sha256": "677e869acdebac4c68091e789f44088b14725a355381080260364e1ba4950ebd",
          "start": 7759,
          "end": 7999,
          "quote": "The first type of knowledge\nis declarative knowledge.\nAnd those are things\nlike statements of fact.\nAnd this is where my\nemail came into play.\nIf you read it all\nthe way to the bottom,\nyou would have entered a raffle.\nSo a statement of fact",
          "topic": "陈述性知识",
          "claim": "Ana Bell 介绍第一种知识是陈述性知识，即事实陈述；她提到如果学生读到她邮件的底部，就会参加一次抽奖。",
          "evidence_indices": [
            336,
            337,
            338,
            339,
            340,
            341,
            342,
            343,
            344,
            345
          ],
          "attribution": "source",
          "importance": "support",
          "attribution_start": null,
          "attribution_end": null
        },
        {
          "id": "F2_1",
          "source_id": "part2",
          "source_sha256": "f93de099f8cbf3578a6ca1fa84294bfe67a979103d1d34f4aa33ddcd9791eda3",
          "start": 8020,
          "end": 8319,
          "quote": "is, someone will win a\nprize before class ends.\nAnd the prize was\na Google Cardboard.\nGoogle state-of-the-art\nvirtual reality glasses.\nAnd I have them right here.\nI delivered on my promise.\nThat's a statement of fact.\nSo pretend I'm a machine.\nOK?\nI don't know anything\nexcept what you tell me.",
          "topic": "课程开场兑现承诺与机器视角",
          "claim": "讲师在课前兑现了承诺，有人将在下课之前赢得一副Google Cardboard虚拟现实眼镜；讲师随后让听众把自己当作一台机器，除了听众告诉他的内容之外什么都不知道。",
          "evidence_indices": [
            2,
            3,
            4,
            5,
            6,
            7,
            8,
            10,
            11,
            12,
            13,
            14,
            15
          ],
          "attribution": "source",
          "importance": "support",
          "attribution_start": null,
          "attribution_end": null
        },
        {
          "id": "F2_2",
          "source_id": "part2",
          "source_sha256": "f93de099f8cbf3578a6ca1fa84294bfe67a979103d1d34f4aa33ddcd9791eda3",
          "start": 8465,
          "end": 8841,
          "quote": "That's where imperative\nknowledge comes in.\nImperative knowledge is\nthe recipe, or the how-to,\nor the sequence of steps.\nSo the sequence of steps\nis imperative knowledge.\nIf I'm a machine,\nyou need to tell me\nhow someone will win a Google\nCardboard before class.\nIf I follow these\nsteps, then technically,\nI should reach a conclusion.",
          "topic": "命令式知识的定义",
          "claim": "命令式知识是配方、操作指南或步骤序列；如果讲师作为机器遵循这些步骤，理论上就能得出结论。",
          "evidence_indices": [
            23,
            24,
            25,
            26,
            27,
            31,
            32,
            33,
            34,
            35,
            36,
            37,
            38,
